docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:6308:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure showing a computational model and benchmark results for single-cell perturbation response prediction.\n\nFigure type: workflow schematic plus UMAP/scatter embeddings and bar plots.\n\nPanels and labels:\n- Panel A: workflow for a “C2S-Scale Perturbation Model,” taking control inputs such as single-cell data and bulk RNA, supporting drug/cytokine/genetic KO perturbations, generating predicted responses, and evaluating against ground truth by gene-program similarity.\n- Panel B: embedding plots comparing “Ground Truth,” “C2S,” “scGen,” “scGPT,” and “CellOT” across perturbation settings including unseen combinations, CD4 T cells IFNγ chronic, and B cells IFNβ + IL-6 chronic. Legend marks untreated ground truth in yellow and treated/generated cells in blue.\n- Panel C: cytokine stimulation benchmark bar plots for scFID, MMD, and Wasserstein-2 distance, comparing C2S-scale 410M, C2S-scale 1B, scGen, CellOT, scGPT, ConDor, BioLord, and linear models.\n- Panel D: cytokine stimulation response plots for IFN response and IL1000 apoptosis response, showing scFID, Kendall tau, and Pearson r for SFT vs GRPO at 410M and 1B model scales.\n- Panel E: SciPlex benchmark bar plots for scFID, MMD, and Wasserstein-1, comparing C2S-scale 410M/1B against scGPT, chemCPA, and ConDor.\n\nMain visible finding: C2S-scale models, especially the 1B version, show lower distributional error metrics than comparison methods in several benchmarks, while reinforcement learning/GRPO appears to improve correlation metrics over SFT in panel D.",
