docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:8140:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel workflow/model architecture figure for a biomedical AI paper. Panels are labeled **a–d**.\n\n- **Panel a:** Compares two strategies: training a foundation model from scratch from single-omics data using tokenization, LLM architecture, and pretraining, versus fine-tuning existing NLP LLMs for CellHermes using multiple omics data, textualization, and fine-tuning.\n- **Panel b:** Shows emulation of self-supervised learning with question-answer prompts. Includes gene expression data converted into masked cell sequences for **MLM in sequence** and **autoregressive sequence** tasks, plus a protein-protein network used for **mask node prediction in graph** and **mask link prediction in graph**. Reconstructed cells/graphs are shown on the right.\n- **Panel c:** Shows human-AI collaboration for prompt design, creating AI-generated diverse prompts from human-written prompts, then CellHermes training via instruction datasets and open-source LLMs such as Llama, Qwen, and DeepSeek with LoRA instruction fine-tuning.\n- **Panel d:** Shows CellHermes applications as an encoder, predictor, and explainer. Inputs include gene/cell text prompts, embeddings, biological databases, perturbation response, cell fitness, gene interaction, and natural-language explanations.\n\nNo quantitative axes or plotted experimental measurements are visible; the main content is a conceptual workflow describing CellHermes training tasks and downstream uses.",
