docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:11865:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific schematic and results figure describing **GEMGen**, a generative model for compound design guided by cell-state gene expression phenotypes.\n\nPanels include:\n- **a:** Concept diagram showing compounds shifting a diseased cell-state landscape toward healthy.\n- **b:** Workflow from an instruction prompt specifying cell type and up/down gene lists, through GEMGen generation, SMILES outputs, filtering, matching score, and experimental/clinical validation.\n- **c:** Model architecture schematic: general-purpose language model adapted to scientific foundation model, with modules for bioentity representation generation, phenotype-guided compound generation, and compound-phenotype matching.\n- **d:** Feature extraction workflow from transcriptomes to differentially expressed genes and ranked DEG lists, with labels for quantitative feature levels and robustness.\n- **e:** Two density plots comparing cosine similarity distributions for random pairs vs duplications: DEG fold changes and DEG embeddings, with W-distance values shown.\n- **f:** Training/evaluation setup listing L1000 compound-phenotype pairs, held-out compounds, single-cell chemical perturbations, genetic perturbation, and cell-state transition.\n\nMain visible finding: the figure presents GEMGen as a phenotype-guided compound generation framework using DEG-based cell-state representations, with embedding-based DEG features appearing more robustly separated for duplicated profiles than raw fold-change features.",
