docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:16401:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure showing a biomedical AI model and its applications.\n\nFigure type: workflow/model architecture and task overview diagram.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Panel **a**: circular atlas-style overview of datasets/modules across tissues/species, with labels such as Mouse-Atlas, DSF, CPCG, CTA, CTX, and tissue names like lung, spleen, blood, pancreas, skin, liver, marrow, thymus.\n- Panel **b**: architecture diagram of a **Q-Former module**, **pre-trained LM**, and **cell reconstruction module**, showing gene expression profiles, learnable queries, self-attention/cross-attention/feed-forward blocks, text tokens, encoder/decoder, latent space, and recovered target gene profile.\n- Panel **c**: three application workflows: **conditional pseudo-cell generation**, **cell type annotation**, and **drug sensitivity prediction**, each framed as a text-based interaction using required/optional attributes such as tissue, species, cell type, sequencing protocol, drug, and single-cell gene expression.\n- Panel **d**: prompt-generation/task simulation schematic, including user traits such as personality, motivation, proficiency, and task-related attributes like cell type, species, tissue, and sequencing protocol.\n\nBiological/model quantity shown: single-cell gene expression profiles and associated metadata used by a language-model-based system for cell reconstruction, annotation, pseudo-cell generation, and drug response prediction.\n\nMain visible finding/message: the figure presents a multimodal/language-model framework for integrating text instructions with single-cell gene expression profiles, enabling multiple single-cell analysis tasks across tissues, species, protocols, and cell types.",
