docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:18514:        "caption": "Figure 3 | Evidence of emergent zero-shot multimodal medical reasoning with Med-PaLM M. Large Med-PaLM M models exhibit zero-shot CoT reasoning capability in identifying and describing tuberculosis related findings in chest X-ray images. The model is prompted with task-specific instructions and a text-only exemplar (without the corresponding image) to generate a report describing findings in the given X-ray image. Model predictions from Med-PaLM M 84B and 562B are shown together with the annotations from an expert radiologist. Both models correctly localized the major TB related cavitory lesion in the right upper lobe. However, both models did not address the small cavitory lesion in left upper lobe (Med-PaLM M 562B was considered better than Med-PaLM M 64B in this example as it also alluded to the opacity in the right middle lobe and did not make the incorrect statement of left lung being clear). Notably, Med-PaLM M 12B failed to generate a coherent report, indicating the importance of scaling for zero-shot COT reasoning.",
docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:18515:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nFigure type: screenshot-style evaluation panel from a biomedical AI/radiology paper.\n\nVisible content: A chest X-ray is shown on the left, with prompt text at the top asking whether it is a normal chest X-ray. On the right are model responses labeled “Med-PaLM M 84B” and “Med-PaLM M 562B,” each followed by correctness/incorrectness annotations.\n\nQuantity/task shown: Qualitative visual question answering evaluation for tuberculosis vs normal chest X-rays, comparing generated model explanations against radiology findings.\n\nMain visible finding: The models identify a right upper-lobe cavitary lesion, but the annotations note missed findings, especially a small left upper-lobe cavitary lesion and a right middle/lower chest opacity likely related to TB.",
