docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:19897:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure showing a multimodal generative foundation model workflow and dataset/model architecture for H&E histopathology.\n\n(a) Conceptual workflow diagram with legend: dark blue arrows mean “generated From H&E,” cyan arrows mean “To generate H&E.” It links RNA/bulk-spatial transcriptomics, proteomics outputs/IHC/MIF, cellular scale/cells, tissue H&E, and text clinical/pathology reports across a scale axis from nm molecular to patient. Central model is labeled “Generative Foundation Model” with “Multimodal Diffusion Model (SiT)” and “Bridging modality” over the H&E tissue image.\n\n(b) Three horizontal bar charts summarizing dataset scale by tissue/site. Left: “H&E images: 100 M,” x-axis number of images in millions, with lung and breast among the largest categories. Middle: “Text-image pairs: 1.6 M,” x-axis thousands, with breast, lung, kidney largest. Right: “RNA-image pairs: 10.8 M,” x-axis thousands, with breast, brain, lung largest.\n\n(c) Model schematic labeled “MUPAD.” It shows multimodal guidance inputs Image/Text/RNA conditioning noisy latent input through multimodal DiT blocks to produce a clean H&E image, with a pretrained encoder and alignment CNN. The inset “Inside a DiT Block” shows self-attention, MLP, timestep input, and decoupled cross-modal attention modules for image, text, and RNA with modality IDs.\n\nMain visible finding/message: the figure presents a multimodal diffusion/DiT framework for generating and translating H&E histology using image, text, RNA, proteomics, and cellular-scale inputs, supported by large multi-tissue H&E, text-image, and RNA-image datasets.",
