docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:20885:        "caption": "Figure 1: OmicsLM overview. Top: example questions from diverse omics datasets are formatted as multi-task conversations for OmicsLM, with each <omics> placeholder denoting a biological profile and gene symbols treated as explicit tokens. Bottom: each placeholder is built by mapping a single-cell or bulk expression profile, including profiles from perturbation screens, to a continuous vector and projecting it into the LLM token-embedding space, allowing multiple profiles to be interleaved in one prompt for sample-level and comparative reasoning.",
docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:20886:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nWorkflow/model architecture schematic for **OmicsLM**, a language model using explicit gene and omics tokenization. The figure has two main sections: a top “multi-task questions” panel showing example biomedical prompts from HCA, TCGA, DEPMAP, and GEO-OMICQA, where `<omics>` tokens are inserted into text; and a right model box labeled “OmicsLM” with token types `text`, `<omics>`, and `gene`.\n\nThe bottom “<omics> token construction” panel shows an omics profile from single-cell or bulk data converted into a continuous profile vector with features such as `countTPM`, `expression`, `FunomicsTO`, and `Geneformer`, then passed through a linear omics projector from \\(R^D\\) to \\(R^H\\), replacing `<omics>` in the LLM input.\n\nMain visible point: the figure illustrates how omics profiles are embedded as tokens so the LLM can handle tasks such as cell typing, cancer phenotyping, perturbation effects, marker discovery, pathway reasoning, tissue annotation, and omics QA.",
