docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:23489:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure showing model evaluation and embedding analyses for nucleotide sequence classification.\n\nPanels include:\n- **A**: Table comparing models **OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, OmniNA-66M, OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, OmniNA-66M** across tasks: nucleotide sequence classification, taxonomy classification, and enzyme classification. Metrics shown are **precision, recall, F1**.\n- **B**: Example nucleotide inputs with colored tokenization segments and response labels, comparing models such as **OmniNA-1.7B**, **OmniNA-330M**, and **OmniNA-66M**.\n- **C, D**: Line plots for **taxonomy** and **gene function**, showing **token number** versus **token frequency**, plus summary token number values.\n- **E, G**: Boxplots of **cosine similarity**, comparing OmniNA models against a randomized/control condition.\n- **F**: t-SNE embedding plot with labeled biological categories such as **mRNA of dehydrogenase**, **mRNA of oxidase**, **chloroplast CDS/mRNA**, and **NLS complex subunit 2**.\n- **H**: t-SNE embedding plot for **HIV1 genes** with a color legend, plus a gene-structure schematic and example model responses.\n\nMain visible finding: OmniNA models appear to produce biologically meaningful tokenization and embedding structure, with higher cosine similarity and clustered t-SNE representations corresponding to gene or functional categories.",
