docs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:26002:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure showing a protein-focused AI model evaluation and workflow. Panels include task schematics, bar plots, and model comparison diagrams.\n\nFigure type: workflow/model architecture plus benchmark results.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- **a**: Question-answering task schematic for ProCyon. Input includes a protein sequence/structure and a disease description; output is yes/no association.\n- **b**: Six horizontal bar charts comparing methods across tasks: protein function, cellular component, biological pathway, disease association, drug target, and drug-protein interaction. X-axis is **Accuracy**. Methods include BLAST-kNN, ESM2-kNN/MLP, ESM3-kNN/MLP, GearNet-kNN/MLP, ProtLLM, and ProCyon.\n- **c**: Bar chart comparing ProCyon-Instruct split performance across many-shot, few-shot, and zero-shot settings for the same task categories. X-axis is **Accuracy**.\n- **d**: Architecture/input comparison showing ProCyon uses sequence and structure tokens, while conventional LLM inputs rely on text identifiers such as HGNC gene symbol, UniProt ID, or full sequence. Indicates generalization to new proteins.\n- **e**: Accuracy bar charts comparing Llama-HG, Mistral-HG, GPT4-HG, and ProCyon for Function, Pathway, and Disease.\n- **f**: Phenotype generation task schematic, where the model generates a mechanism-of-action style textual phenotype description from a protein and drug indication.\n- **g**: Bar charts comparing **BERTScore** for out-of-vocabulary generalization across Function, Pathway, and Disease.\n- **h**: Bar charts showing win rate versus ProCyon for small, medium, and frontier models across Function, Pathway, and Disease.\n\nMain visible finding: ProCyon generally performs competitively or best across protein-related QA, annotation, association, and phenotype-generation benchmarks, with especially strong results in tasks involving drug targets, drug-protein interaction, and out-of-vocabulary/generalization settings.",
